      question: "Was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand the main cause of World War One? (16 marks)",
      answer: "The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 was the immediate trigger for WWI but it must be seen in the context of long-term causes that made war likely. The assassination lit the fuse, but the gunpowder had been accumulating for decades. Long-term causes: militarism — European powers had been building arms for decades; the Anglo-German naval rivalry created suspicion. Alliance systems — the Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy) and Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) meant a local conflict could escalate globally. Imperialism — competition for colonies had created repeated crises (Moroccan Crises 1905, 1911). Nationalism — pan-Slavic nationalism in the Balkans threatened Austria-Hungary's multi-ethnic empire; Alsace-Lorraine remained a source of Franco-German tension. These factors made war much more likely than it would have been without the assassination. However, the assassination was more than a coincidental spark — it activated the alliance system and gave Austria-Hungary a pretext to attack Serbia. The July Crisis (ultimatums, mobilisation decisions, Schlieffen Plan) shows how political decisions in summer 1914 turned the assassination into a world war. In conclusion, the assassination was the necessary immediate trigger but not the main underlying cause — the alliance system and long-term tensions made European war almost inevitable. Another incident might have started the war even without Sarajevo.",
